<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  macOS hardened-runtime entitlements. WRITTEN, NEVER BUILT: nothing in this
  repository has been signed or notarized, because there is no Mac and no Apple
  Developer ID here. README.md "What was verified, and what was only configured".

  WHY EACH ONE IS HERE, AND WHY THE OBVIOUS ONES ARE NOT:

  · allow-jit / allow-unsigned-executable-memory — ONNX Runtime Web runs the
    model in WebAssembly, and V8 and the wasm engine both write executable
    memory. Without these two the engine renderer is killed by the kernel the
    moment it compiles anything, which looks like "the app crashes when you
    press Arm".

  · allow-dyld-environment-variables / disable-library-validation — Electron's
    own helper processes and its framework bundle. electron-builder documents
    both as required for a hardened-runtime Electron app.

  THERE IS NO MICROPHONE ENTITLEMENT, and that is the product. This app captures
  what the embedded YouTube view RENDERS, through getDisplayMedia on a
  WebContentsView we own (docs/spike-capture-mute.md) — it never opens an input
  device. `com.apple.security.device.audio-input` would be an entitlement for a
  capability the product does not have, and asking for one is how an app ends up
  showing the user a microphone prompt it cannot explain.

  THERE IS NO NETWORK-CLIENT EXCEPTION EITHER: the hardened runtime does not gate
  outbound sockets, and rule P1' (src/main/netguard.js, CONTRIBUTING.md) is what
  binds this app's own traffic to exactly one host.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
